# SESAME remotes for Rust Yon projects

**Same-host / development only: Rust Yon handlers at TACHYON `a7ccbfff` have no
TLS-capable HTTP dependency (`crates/tachyon-core/src/handler/source.rs:1553-1560`
builds them with the `http` crate alone), so this client cannot reach an https
SESAME.** It talks plain HTTP to a SESAME on the same machine
(`localhost`, `127.0.0.1` or `[::1]`) and refuses every other origin, https
included, before it opens a connection. Do not use it to reach SESAME across a
network. Rust is not a production-supported SESAME client language.

These files let a Tachyon Yon project written in Rust call SESAME's HTTP API.
They follow Tachyon's remotes format: one bodiless `#[HTTPClient]` trait per
route family in `server/remotes/`, one `SesameSetup` that registers the
`sesame` HTTP adapter, and one `SesameTransportHelper` that sends the requests
to SESAME over a standard-library TCP connection.

The files are generated by `go run ./tools/remotes/generate` from
[`api/machine/v1/operations.json`](../../api/machine/v1/operations.json). Do
not edit them here: change `tools/remotes/templates/rust/` and run the
generator again.

## Requirements

- `ty` 26.36.01 (commit `a7ccbfff`) or later, and Rust with `cargo`.
- A SESAME Yon host on the same machine, listening on a loopback address (see
  [docs/YON.md](../../docs/YON.md)).

## Copy them into your project

```bash
cp -R remotes/rust/server/. /path/to/your-project/server/
```

This adds `server/remotes/Sesame*Client.rs`, `server/setups/SesameSetup.rs`
and `server/helpers/SesameTransportHelper.rs`.

## Configure

Add these members to `server/application.json`:

```json
{
  "outgoing": { "hosts": ["127.0.0.1"] },
  "sesame": { "origin": "http://127.0.0.1:8080" },
  "names": { "words": ["authn", "deprovision", "dpop", "jwks", "oidc", "saml", "scim", "totp"] },
  "duplication": { "check": false }
}
```

- `sesame.origin`, or `YON_SESAME_ORIGIN` in the project's `.env`: the local
  SESAME's origin, `http://` with `localhost`, `127.0.0.1` or `[::1]` and an
  optional port, without path, query, fragment or user name. With any other
  value, `https` included, every call fails without sending anything.
- `outgoing.hosts`: the loopback address of `sesame.origin`. Tachyon sends
  outgoing requests through a guard that refuses every host not in this list
  (Tachyon ADR 0068). The transport reads Tachyon's `HTTP_PROXY` setting and
  sends every request through that guard.
- `names.words`: the remote class and method names use these SESAME words,
  and Tachyon's name check (`TY1328`) refuses them otherwise.
- `duplication.check`: every contract repeats the same method shape, and
  Tachyon's duplication check (`TY1315`) reports it, although a contract has
  no code to share. Until Tachyon skips bodiless contracts, the project builds
  only with this check turned off.

## Call SESAME

Inject a remote into a service with `#[Needs]`:

```rust
#[yon::service]
struct SignInService {
  #[Needs]
  sign_in: std::sync::Arc<SesameAuthnClient>,
}
impl SignInService {
    fn begin(&self, tenant_id: BehaviorValue, email: BehaviorValue) -> BehaviorResult<BehaviorValue> {
        self.sign_in.begin(BehaviorValue::object([
            ("tenant_id", tenant_id),
            ("identifier_namespace", BehaviorValue::from("email")),
            ("identifier_value", email),
        ]))
    }
}
```

Arguments and answers are `BehaviorValue` JSON values. Tachyon does not decode
them into types, so check the fields you read. The request bodies are the
operation parameters described in [docs/YON.md](../../docs/YON.md) and
[api/machine/v1](../../api/machine/v1/README.md). Every `POST` method takes a
body: send an empty `BehaviorValue::Object` when the route reads no fields.

`principal.suspend` is the method `suspend_principal`: Tachyon reads a method
named `suspend` as asynchronous and the handler then fails to compile
(`TY2004`, `await` outside an `async` function).

## Authentication headers

- A method for a `signed_in` or `administrator` route takes `session` first.
  Pass `BehaviorValue::from("<session_id>:<session_secret>")` of the person
  your application acts for; it is sent as the `Sesame-Session` header.
- A method for a SCIM provisioning route (`scim/user-*`, `scim/group-*`) takes
  `authorization` first. Pass `"Bearer <token>"`; it is sent as the
  `Authorization` header.
- Methods for `public` routes take no header.

Never log these values and never put them in a path or a query. An empty
string sends an empty header, and SESAME answers `401`.

## Answers and errors

Every method answers `{"status": <HTTP status>, "body": <JSON body or null>}`.
Check `status` before you read `body`:

- `2xx`: `body` is SESAME's result.
- SESAME's own refusals: `body` is `{"code": "<code>"}`, with the status from
  the table in [docs/YON.md](../../docs/YON.md#errors) (`400`, `401`, `403`,
  `404`, `409`, `503` for `engine_busy`: retry later, `500` for
  `internal_error`).
- The route guard of SESAME's `ty` answers `401`, `403` and `500` as plain
  text, so `body` is `null`.
- `ty`'s request checks answer `400` with `{"error", "field", "part"}`.

The call itself returns `Err(BehaviorError)` with the code
`adapter` when `sesame.origin` is missing or not a loopback `http` origin, the network fails,
the deadline passes, SESAME answers with a redirect (never followed), or the
answer is larger than 1 MiB. Tachyon hides the cause on purpose. With the
`http` loopback origin, a host missing from `outgoing.hosts`
answers `403` from Tachyon's guard instead.

## What the remotes do not cover

- Standard endpoints: only `GET /oidc/openid-configuration` and
  `GET /oidc/jwks` (`SesameStandardsClient`). `/oidc/authorize` and
  `/oidc/logout` are browser redirects, and `/oidc/token`, `/oidc/introspect`
  and `/oidc/revoke` take form-encoded bodies, which a Tachyon remote cannot
  send. Use an OAuth 2.0 and OpenID Connect client library with the discovery
  document for them.
- `oidc/interaction-get` and `oidc/interaction-complete` read the browser's
  `__Secure-sesame_interaction` cookie. Tachyon refuses a `Cookie` header in a
  remote, so through a remote they answer as if the cookie were missing. Call
  them from the browser.
- Operations that SESAME never serves over HTTP: `tenant.bootstrap` and
  `admin.bootstrap` (command line only) and `federation.login_exchange`.

